<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · West Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Bamum</h1><p class="st-meta">1394 CE – 1923 CE (approx.) · capital Foumban</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Grassfields kingdom of western Cameroon whose king Ibrahim Njoya invented a script, a religion and a palace culture still admired today.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Bamum tradition traces the dynasty to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nchare-yen">Nchare Yen</a>, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tikar-people">Tikar</a> prince who conquered the area around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/foumban">Foumban</a> and became its first Mfon, traditionally in 1394. The kingdom remained small until the late 18th or early 19th century, when Mbuembue expanded it greatly and fortified the capital with a deep ditch.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Mfon ruled from a large palace at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/foumban">Foumban</a> with titled palace servants and councils of nobles. Bamum was famous for brass casting, beadwork and carved thrones. King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibrahim-njoya">Ibrahim Njoya</a>, who came to the throne in the late 1880s, created the Bamum script, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/printing-press">printing</a> press, maps and a syncretic religion blending <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> and local belief.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Bamum came under <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kamerun">German Kamerun</a> around 1902 and then <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/french-cameroon">French Cameroon</a> after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>. The French curtailed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibrahim-njoya">Njoya</a>'s power in the 1920s and exiled him in 1931. The dynasty survives as a sultanate at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/foumban">Foumban</a>, whose palace houses a renowned museum.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kingdom-of-bamum.jpg" alt="Kingdom of Bamum" width="320" height="322"><figcaption>Image: Hafenreffer Museum of Brown University · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bamun_Kingdom.gif" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1394 CE – 1923 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Foumban</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Nchare Yen, Mbuembue, Ibrahim Njoya</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Islam and Bamum traditional religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Bamum</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Cameroon</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Bamum, Bamoun, Kingdom of Bamoun</dd></dl></aside></div>

